				function rawContent() {
					return "\nThe union bug is one of the most recognized labor solidarity symbols in print — and one of the least understood by people outside the labor movement. For campaigns, political candidates, union organizations, and government contractors, it can be a non-negotiable requirement. For others, it's simply a mark of quality and labor accountability.\n\nAt [Cornerstone Services](/services/union-printing) in New Paltz, we provide union-label printing for clients across the Hudson Valley who need certified union work. Here's what the union bug is, what it means, and how to make sure your printed materials carry it correctly.\n\n## The History of the Union Bug\n\nThe union bug originated with the International Typographical Union (ITU), founded in 1852 — one of the oldest trade unions in North America. As labor organizing grew through the late 19th century, unions developed the bug as a way for consumers and organizations to identify printed materials produced under union labor conditions.\n\nThe concept was straightforward: a union that wanted to demonstrate labor solidarity could verify that the printed materials they distributed — newsletters, flyers, ballots, campaign literature — were produced by workers covered by a union contract. The bug on the piece was the verifiable mark.\n\nOver time, the printing industry's union landscape consolidated. Today, the primary printing industry union is the **Graphic Communications Conference of the International Brotherhood of Teamsters (GCC/IBT)**, which merged with the Teamsters in 2005. The union bug used on contemporary printing typically reflects GCC/IBT local affiliation.\n\n## What the Bug Contains and Where It Goes\n\nA standard union bug is a small typeset block — typically 1/8 to 1/4 inch — that includes:\n- The union name and local number\n- A brief tagline (historically \"All Union\")\n- Sometimes the shop identification\n\nIt appears most commonly:\n- In the lower corner of the front face of a printed piece\n- On the address side of a direct mail piece\n- In the imprint area of political campaign literature\n- In the legal information block of government-required printed materials\n\nThe exact formatting requirements vary by union local and by client requirements. Political campaign materials often have specific legal placement requirements (paid-for-by line, union bug, and printer identification) that all must appear together.\n\n## Who Requires the Union Bug\n\n### Labor Organizations\n\nAny union producing printed materials for internal distribution — newsletters, meeting notices, ballots, contract summaries — typically requires the union bug as a matter of solidarity. A union distributing materials printed by non-union labor sends a contradictory message to its membership.\n\n### Political Campaigns Targeting Union Voters\n\nPolitical campaigns courting union endorsements or targeting union voters frequently use union-bug-certified printing for all campaign materials. In competitive districts with significant union membership — particularly in manufacturing, trades, government, and service sectors — the absence of the union bug is noticed by union household recipients.\n\nIn the Hudson Valley and lower Hudson Valley, the density of union households in certain communities makes union-bug-certified campaign mail a tactical consideration, not just a symbolic one.\n\n### Government Contracts with Union Labor Provisions\n\nSome government printing contracts, particularly at the municipal and county level, include provisions requiring union labor. Municipalities, school districts, or government agencies with these requirements need a print vendor who can certify union-label production.\n\n### Organizations with Union Employee Bases\n\nAny organization with unionized employees may choose to use union-label printing as a demonstration of alignment with its workforce's values — hospitals, universities, public agencies.\n\n## How to Verify Union Affiliation\n\nA print shop that claims union affiliation should be able to provide:\n- The name and local number of the affiliated union\n- Contact information for the local to verify current affiliation\n- A copy of their collective bargaining agreement or a letter from the local confirming active affiliation\n\nThe union bug itself is a trademark — using it without active affiliation is infringement. Clients who need to document union-label printing for compliance purposes (government contracts, union endorsement requirements) should request written documentation from the printer.\n\n## Common Mistakes with Union Bug Placement\n\nThe most frequent issue we see at Cornerstone is clients who wait until the proof stage to mention they need the union bug. By that point, the layout is finalized, and fitting the bug — plus required legal lines like the paid-for-by attribution on political mail — into the remaining space creates design compromises.\n\nThe second most common problem is assuming any print shop can add a union bug after the fact. The bug is not clip art. It is a registered mark tied to a specific union local, and it can only appear on work produced by that local's affiliated shop. If your initial print run was produced by a non-union vendor, there is no way to retroactively add a legitimate union bug.\n\nFor Hudson Valley campaigns and labor organizations that need certified union printing: specify the union bug requirement at the quoting stage, not the proof stage. It affects vendor selection, layout planning, and compliance documentation from day one.\n\n## Union Bug Variations and Related Labels\n\nThe union bug is not the only labor-related label used in printing and related industries. Understanding the differences helps clients specify exactly what they need:\n\n**GCC/IBT bug:** The most common printing union bug in the United States, representing the Graphic Communications Conference of the International Brotherhood of Teamsters. This is the standard for commercial printing, including direct mail postcards, brochures, letters, and campaign materials.\n\n**Allied Printing Trades Council (APTC) label:** A broader label used when multiple printing trades unions were involved in producing a piece. Less common today due to union consolidation, but still referenced in some union contracts and government specifications.\n\n**\"Printed in the USA\" label:** Not a union label — this indicates domestic production but says nothing about labor standards. A non-union shop printing domestically can use \"Printed in the USA.\" Do not confuse this with the union bug.\n\n**AFL-CIO \"Union Made\" label:** A general labor label indicating the product was made by union workers. Used more commonly in manufacturing than in printing, but some labor organizations request both the specific GCC/IBT bug and the broader AFL-CIO label on their materials.\n\n**\"Bug-free\" or \"clean\" printing:** Industry shorthand for non-union-produced printing. Some campaigns and organizations that do not need or want union certification specify \"no bug required\" in their print specifications.\n\n## The Union Bug in Digital Printing\n\nThe rise of digital printing (as opposed to traditional offset printing) has created some complexity around the union bug. Historically, the bug applied to work done by union press operators on offset printing presses. Digital printing uses different equipment and different skill sets.\n\nIn practice, the applicability of the union bug to digitally printed materials depends on the union local's coverage and the shop's collective bargaining agreement. At Cornerstone, our digital printing work is covered under our union agreement and carries the same bug as our offset work. Not all union shops operate this way — some shops have union coverage for offset operations only, with digital work performed by non-covered employees.\n\nCampaigns and organizations that need the union bug on digitally printed materials (short-run postcards, variable data letters, personalized campaign mail) should confirm with the print shop that their digital printing is covered under the union agreement.\n\n## Requesting Union-Bug-Certified Printing: A Client Checklist\n\nWhen ordering union-bug-certified printing, provide or confirm the following at the quote stage:\n\n1. **Specify the union bug requirement** in your initial request or RFP — do not assume the shop will add it automatically\n2. **Request documentation** of the shop's union local affiliation — the local name, number, and a contact at the local who can verify\n3. **Confirm placement** of the bug in your design layout — provide a designated area in the imprint block for the bug and any legal attribution text\n4. **Verify coverage** of the specific production method — if the job involves digital printing, confirm the digital work is covered by the union agreement\n5. **Request a proof** showing the bug in position before approving the final print run — verify the bug format, size, and placement are correct\n\n## Common Misconceptions About the Union Bug\n\nSeveral persistent misunderstandings about the union bug cause problems for campaigns and organizations:\n\n**\"Any printer can add a union bug.\"** False. The bug is a registered mark that can only be used by shops affiliated with the specific union local. A non-union shop cannot legally place a union bug on its work, and doing so could constitute fraud.\n\n**\"The union bug is just a logo.\"** The bug is a certification mark — it certifies that the work was performed by union labor under a collective bargaining agreement. It carries legal and ethical weight beyond a decorative element.\n\n**\"Union printing always costs more.\"** Not necessarily. Union shops compete on price like any other business. The labor cost difference between union and non-union shops in the printing industry is typically 5–15%, and many union shops offset this through efficiency, modern equipment, and volume. For government contracts with prevailing wage requirements, the cost difference may be negligible.\n\n**\"The bug goes anywhere on the piece.\"** The bug has conventional placement — typically in the lower-left corner or the imprint area of the piece. Placement should be visible but not distracting. It should not be resized to the point of illegibility or placed over other design elements.\n\n**\"You can add the bug after the fact.\"** The bug must be printed as part of the production process at the union-affiliated shop. It cannot be stamped, stickered, or digitally added to a piece printed elsewhere.\n\nAt Cornerstone Services in New Paltz, we can provide union-label documentation for jobs that require it. Call [(845) 255-5722](tel:8452555722) or [request a quote](/estimate) to discuss union-label printing requirements for your project.\n";
				}
